# Feature interview
These original instructions are bundled with Ralph.
Explore the repository with read-only tools before asking questions. Read existing code,
project documentation, and tests. Infer discoverable facts from that evidence.
Ask one unresolved product preference at a time. Recommend a concrete answer and explain
its tradeoff briefly. Resolve scope, acceptance criteria, constraints, and failure behavior.
Use the persisted feature input and transcript. A model statement cannot approve a feature.
When decisions are resolved, explain the agreed feature in short sentences. Use active voice,
consistent terms, and only necessary technical words. Include agreed scope and observable
acceptance criteria. Do not claim a measured score or formal ASD-STE100 compliance.
Return only JSON in one of these forms:
{"kind":"question","question":"...","recommendation":"..."}
{"kind":"explanation","text":"...","scope":["..."],"criteria":["..."]}
Do not write code, generate a PRD, or start implementation.
# Feature interview and explanation approval

## Summary
`/ralph-orchestrator` starts durable planning after a valid command configuration is saved.
Commands may be configured without running setup verification; execution performs its own baseline checks. `startInterview` ends at explanation approval; the public command then continues through PRD/issue planning and combined approval.

## Key behavior
- The canonical Git-common-directory store owns `active-run.lock`, including linked
  worktrees. Planning and its approved explanation retain the lock for downstream phases.
- Main-session questions use `requestUserInput`; pending requests, recommendations,
  answers, typed failures and explanation scope/criteria are journaled.
- Correlation checks bind human message provenance, session, request and revision.
  Assistant prose cannot approve. Content changes recompute the hash, advance the
  artifact revision and invalidate carried-forward approvals and old pending requests.
- Interview jobs use read-only observation sessions and the selected role model.
- Optional `.ralph/project.json` sets role models, skill paths, concurrency (default 2)
  and agent timeout (default 300000 ms). Relative overrides resolve from the workspace.
  Captured instruction hashes must match when continuing; missing skills fail before locking.
- Agent timeout freezes completions, records interruption and cancels the correlated session.
  Human response waits have no timeout.

## Code references
- `src/commands/orchestrator.ts`, `src/commands/router.ts`, `src/plugin.ts` — public entrypoints.
- `src/planning/interview.ts`, `src/planning/types.ts`, `src/planning/artifacts.ts` — conversational state and gate.
- `src/state/run-store.ts` — compare-and-swap persistence and approval invalidation.
- `src/config/project.ts`, `src/agents/skills.ts`, `skills/grill-me/SKILL.md` — configuration and original bundled guidance.
- `src/opencode/adapter.ts` — reads the pinned v2 main-session model selection.
- `tests/integration/03-interview.test.ts` — public workflow demonstration and regressions.

## Related
- [Project commands](project-commands.md)
- [Workflow recovery](workflow-recovery.md)

## Notes
Production input uses real user chat messages with request/session/revision correlation. The visible tool returns at questions and resumes on the next reply; worker interviews remain separate sessions. The selected main-session model is captured for executor, merger and review defaults, with explicit role overrides preserved. Planning recovery remains conservative about ambiguous locks and operations. Native Linux/Windows and interactive TUI layout remain unverified locally.

